
// Thesis — SEMANTIC-LEANING repetition detection.
//
// The old memory (src/Memory.kf) is purely lexical: n-gram overlap over the
// whole text. Measured on 2026-10-11 it let two posts through that argued the
// SAME thesis with different words ("tecnologia antiga nao e debito tecnico" at
// 15:00 and the same idea restated at 18:00 shared 0% of their 4-grams). The
// spec is explicit that lexical comparison is not enough and that a comparison
// proportional to the project is wanted, WITHOUT adding unnecessary
// infrastructure.
//
// So this module compares the THESIS, not the prose, in three layers:
//
//   1. A curated fold maps domain synonyms onto one token, so "microsservico"
//      and "distribuicao", "legado" and "antigo", "hype" and "moda" stop
//      looking like different subjects.
//   2. A signature is the set of distinctive content words (stopwords and
//      generic filler removed), which is stable under rephrasing that keeps
//      the vocabulary and shifts the syntax.
//   3. POLARITY: the same words with opposite stance ("X funciona" vs "X nao
//      funciona") are different theses, so negation/contrast markers are
//      counted and a polarity clash BLOCKS the "same thesis" verdict.
//
// WHAT THIS STILL CANNOT DO, stated plainly: two texts that argue the same
// idea using genuinely different vocabulary ("complexidade acidental" vs
// "overengineering") remain invisible to any deterministic method here. That
// gap is closed only by the OPTIONAL model adjudication below, which is off by
// default and, when on, is asked ONE short yes/no question about two theses —
// a bounded, cheap call, not a second full generation.

// Lowercase + strip Latin-1 accents, mapping every non-word char to a space.
// Inlined (not Memory.foldForMemory) so `kof test src/Thesis.kf` compiles and
// runs standalone — the root-package split forces self-contained modules.
String thesisFoldText(String s) {
    var lower = s.toLowerCase()
    var out = ""
    var i = 0
    while (i < lower.length) {
        var c = lower.charAt(i)
        if ((c >= 97 && c <= 122) || (c >= 48 && c <= 57)) {
            out = out + lower.substring(i, i + 1)
        } else if (c == 224 || c == 225 || c == 226 || c == 227 || c == 229) {
            out = out + "a"
        } else if (c == 231) {
            out = out + "c"
        } else if (c == 232 || c == 233 || c == 234) {
            out = out + "e"
        } else if (c == 237) {
            out = out + "i"
        } else if (c == 242 || c == 243) {
            out = out + "o"
        } else if (c == 250) {
            out = out + "u"
        } else {
            out = out + " "
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out
}

// ---- stopwords and filler ----
//
// Not just connectives: also the words that appear in EVERY post of this
// author ("engenharia", "tecnico", "software") carry no thesis signal, so
// keeping them would make every pair of texts look similar.

List<String> thesisStopwords() {
    var w = listOf<String>()
    w.add("a")
    w.add("o")
    w.add("as")
    w.add("os")
    w.add("de")
    w.add("da")
    w.add("do")
    w.add("das")
